 yeah  we could we could not go barefoot and a  month that had our I never understood  that but that was my mother's rule yeah  so she would let you go barefoot when it  was warm outside and those were the  months that didn't have an arm yeah  did you ever hear about hookworm when  you might have heard about it yes I  don't recall ever anybody ever having it  that I knew but I'm sure my mom she  mentioned people that had it but I  didn't know them you know do you feel  like there was any stigma around people  who had hookworm like what did other  people think of a person if they had  hookworm but they were dirty  they were dirty that they were walking  through poop in the dirt yeah and they  didn't take baths every day like they do  now  you know if they took a bath on Saturday  in the summertime that was probably it  you know so yeah they they got it from  the dirt and not washing hands and  you're doing stuff like that and so of  course we know today that hookworm only  burrows through the feet it doesn't  really have anything to do with  hand-washing or how often you bathe  because the hookworm can burrow into the  soles of your feet in just a couple of  minutes so you could still take a bath  every day but if you've walked barefoot  through a contaminated area then you can  still get hookworm so just kind of goes  to show some of the stigma that can be  and some of those sort of misinformation  that can surround something like that  of course we never hear about hook room  anymore but if there's a hookworm in the  United States it turns out that there is  for a long time it was thought that  hookworm had more or less than  eradicated in the United States and now  just within the last maybe three or four  years it's been determined that there's  still hookworm and especially poverty  stricken areas in the extreme southeast  we're talking about mainly I think  Alabama and Mississippi in very very  poor communities again where there's no  indoor plumbing where peop